// SAT Linux frontend on Qt Widgets — phase L0 shell.
//
// SCOPE OF THIS FILE TODAY
//
// This is the L0 scaffolding described in docs/DESIGN_LINUX_GUI.md. What it proves is the chrome:
// Qt links, the application starts from a self-contained folder with no Qt installed on the host,
// and the window is recognisably Support Assist Tool — same palette, same sidebar, same role badge,
// same Home cards in the same words as the Windows edition.
//
// Working today: the case workspace (create, switch, delete, and the Case Dashboard that shows
// what the active case is and where its evidence lives), Help/About and its articles, the universal
// row commands, the shell keys and the status-bar controls.
//
// NOT working yet: every execution-catalog operation. Those cards are LISTED rather than hidden, so
// the catalog matches the Windows edition, and each one says which phase builds it instead of
// pretending to run. The Xlib frontend became a problem precisely because it looked more finished
// than it was; a shell that is honest about its own state is not the same mistake as a shell that
// lies about it.
//
// WHERE THE APPEARANCE COMES FROM
//
// Nothing here is designed. The palette is in sat_theme.hpp with the Windows brush it came from;
// the sidebar and the Home cards come from workspace_view_model.hpp, which reproduces
// AppWindow::rebuild_navigation() and AppWindow::cards_for_page(). If Windows changes, those two
// places change and this file follows.

